      Quality control for workwear begins with fabric certification testing before cutting. We verify fabric weight, tear strength, color fastness, flame resistance if applicable, and hi-vis reflectivity. Workwear fabrics require specialized testing to ensure they meet safety standards including EN ISO 20471 for hi-vis, EN 11611 for welding protection, and NFPA 2112 for flame resistance. Any fabric that doesn't meet specifications is rejected and replaced. Our fabric suppliers provide full certification documentation, enabling traceability and compliance with international workwear regulations.
    </p>

    <p style="color:#FFFFFF;max-width:800px;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.95rem;margin-top:1.5rem;">
      During production, we implement inline quality checks at multiple stages specific to workwear construction. Cutting supervisors verify pattern accuracy and ensure proper grain direction for consistent sizing. Sewing line leaders monitor stitching quality, seam consistency, and construction standards including reflective tape placement, button attachment strength, and zipper functionality. Our production floor follows ISO 9001 quality management procedures with documented quality checkpoints. Any garment that doesn't meet quality or safety standards during inline inspection is flagged for rework or rejection.
    </p>

    <p style="color:#FFFFFF;max-width:800px;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.95rem;margin-top:1.5rem;">
